    <exercises xml:id="exercises-angle-between-a-line-and-a-plane">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-box-diagonal-and-faces">
            <statement><p>A box <m>ABCDEFGH</m> has <m>AB = 20\,\text{cm}</m>, <m>BC = 12\,\text{cm}</m> and <m>AE = 9\,\text{cm}</m>. Find the angle between <m>AG</m> and (a) the base <m>ABCD</m>; (b) the face <m>BCGF</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>(a) <m>21.1^{\circ}</m> (b) <m>53.1^{\circ}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>(a) The projection is <m>AC = \sqrt{400 + 144} = \sqrt{544}</m>, so <m>\tan GAC = \dfrac{9}{\sqrt{544}}</m> and the angle is about <m>21.1^{\circ}</m>.</p>
            <p>(b) <m>A</m> projects to <m>B</m> (the edge <m>AB</m> is at right angles to the face <m>BCGF</m>), so the projection is <m>BG = \sqrt{144 + 81} = 15\,\text{cm}</m> and the angle is at <m>G</m>: <m>\tan AGB = \dfrac{20}{15}</m>, about <m>53.1^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-rectangular-pyramid-edge">
            <statement><p>A right pyramid <m>VABCD</m> has a rectangular base with <m>AB = 16\,\text{cm}</m> and <m>BC = 12\,\text{cm}</m>, and each sloping edge is <m>14\,\text{cm}</m> long. Find the angle between <m>VA</m> and the base.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>44.4^{\circ}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>AC = \sqrt{256 + 144} = 20</m>, so the projection <m>OA = 10\,\text{cm}</m>. In triangle <m>VOA</m>, <m>\cos VAO = \dfrac{10}{14}</m>, so the angle is about <m>44.4^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-tent-line-and-ground">
            <statement><p>For the tent in <xref ref="fig-tent-prism"/> (<m>AB = 2.4\,\text{m}</m>, <m>CA = CB = 1.5\,\text{m}</m>, <m>AD = 3\,\text{m}</m>), find the angle between the line <m>AF</m> and the ground.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>15.6^{\circ}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>F</m> is above <m>P</m>, the midpoint of <m>DE</m>, at height <m>0.9\,\text{m}</m>. The projection is <m>AP</m>, with <m>AP^2 = 1.2^2 + 3^2 = 10.44</m>. So <m>\tan FAP = \dfrac{0.9}{\sqrt{10.44}}</m> and the angle is about <m>15.6^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-flagpole-wire-angle">
            <statement><p>A vertical flagpole at a school in Nyeri is <m>8\,\text{m}</m> high. A wire runs from its top to a peg on level ground <m>5.5\,\text{m}</m> from its foot. Find the angle between the wire and the ground.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>55.5^{\circ}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>The projection of the wire is the <m>5.5\,\text{m}</m> line from the peg to the foot of the pole. <m>\tan \theta = \dfrac{8}{5.5}</m>, so <m>\theta \approx 55.5^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-corner-pyramid-line-and-planes">
            <statement><p>A pyramid <m>VABC</m> has a right angle <m>BAC</m> in its base, <m>AB = AC = 10\,\text{cm}</m>, and the edge <m>VA = 7\,\text{cm}</m> is vertical. Find the angle between <m>VB</m> and (a) the base <m>ABC</m>; (b) the face <m>VAC</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>(a) <m>35.0^{\circ}</m> (b) <m>55.0^{\circ}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>(a) <m>V</m> is above <m>A</m>, so the projection of <m>VB</m> is <m>AB</m>: <m>\tan VBA = \dfrac{7}{10}</m>, about <m>35.0^{\circ}</m>.</p>
            <p>(b) <m>BA</m> is at right angles to both <m>AC</m> and <m>AV</m>, so it is perpendicular to the face <m>VAC</m>, and <m>B</m> projects to <m>A</m>. The projection of <m>VB</m> is <m>VA</m>: <m>\tan BVA = \dfrac{10}{7}</m>, about <m>55.0^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-projection-gives-smallest-angle">
            <title>Challenge: The Projection Gives the Smallest Angle</title>
            <statement><p>In a cuboid <m>ABCDEFGH</m>, show that angle <m>GAC</m> is smaller than angle <m>GAB</m>.</p></statement>
            <hint><p>Angles <m>ACG</m> and <m>ABG</m> are both right angles. Compare <m>\cos GAC = \dfrac{AC}{AG}</m> with <m>\cos GAB = \dfrac{AB}{AG}</m>.</p></hint>
            <answer><p><m>AC \gt AB</m>, so <m>\cos GAC \gt \cos GAB</m>, so angle <m>GAC</m> is smaller.</p></answer>
            <solution><p>Triangle <m>ACG</m> has a right angle at <m>C</m> and triangle <m>ABG</m> has a right angle at <m>B</m>, so <m>\cos GAC = \dfrac{AC}{AG}</m> and <m>\cos GAB = \dfrac{AB}{AG}</m>. <m>AC</m> is the hypotenuse of triangle <m>ABC</m>, so <m>AC \gt AB</m>, and therefore <m>\cos GAC \gt \cos GAB</m>. For acute angles a larger cosine means a smaller angle, so angle <m>GAC</m> is smaller than angle <m>GAB</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-angle-between-a-line-and-a-plane">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Give a four-step card: where does the line meet the plane; drop a perpendicular; join to find the projection; use the right-angled triangle. Work through the activity and <xref ref="example-diagonal-of-a-water-tank"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-box-diagonal-and-faces"/> (a) and <xref ref="ex-flagpole-wire-angle"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity plus all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-box-diagonal-and-faces"/> to <xref ref="ex-corner-pyramid-line-and-planes"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-projection-gives-smallest-angle"/>. Extension: for which height does a sloping edge of a right pyramid on a square base of side <m>6\,\text{m}</m> make <m>45^{\circ}</m> with the base? (Answer: when the height equals <m>OA = 3\sqrt{2} \approx 4.24\,\text{m}</m>.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

